Usage:
  commons publish [options] [--json]

sign and commit a statement (authored with its bytes, holds, describes,
retracts, or a draft)

Signs with --key (or $COMMONS_KEY) and commits through the store's journal: the
blob and its statements land together or not at all. A draft is never stored
unsigned. Your log's next seq and prev come from the statements this store
holds; a FORK in your log is refused until you name --prev. --intended-for runs
the named edition's rules first and refuses on fail. With availability withheld
and content under 64 bytes it warns that short content may be enumerable against
its hash (a heuristic; include a nonce) and proceeds; the warning is not
recorded.

A rotate_key draft is signed as written: none of `commons rotate`'s checks run
(successor, key events already held, merged tips), so the way out after a
compromise stays open; it warns and proceeds. With --as <identity> --grant <id>
the statement acts for a fixed-root identity (rc/2, the actor in the signed
envelope): it is refused, nothing signed, unless the identity evaluator over the
statements this store holds plus this line reads the act eligible (exit 2 when
it is denied, naming why; exit 3 when it cannot decide, e.g. the grant or the
identity is not held).

A collection's genesis (a draft) names key controllers only: a controller that
is an identity is refused (KNOWN-LIMITS L-21), and an act for an identity on an
edition (--as) needs a grant carrying `edition` for a resource that already
exists. A holds is written beside an authored or observed whose bytes this store
keeps (--no-holds withholds it); it stays your key's own (rc/1): your key holds
the bytes.

External signing (identity profile §8): --prepare <file> --signer <key> writes
an external-signing package and appends nothing; sign its PAE bytes with the key
elsewhere, then --complete <file> --signature <file|-> verifies the signature
over the recomputed bytes, runs every check again, and commits only when your
log has not moved since (else stale-prepare, exit 1). Only an act (--as) is
prepared; an authored act needs --no-holds, and its eligibility is read at
--complete.

Signing options:
  --key <file>              the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is
                            no default
  --prev <id>               the line of your log to extend; needed when your log
                            has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip
  --ts <time>               the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default
                            now; more than 300 s ahead is refused
  --dry-run                 print what would be signed and committed, sign
                            nothing, write nothing

Statement options:
  --draft <file>            a JSON draft {"kind":…,"body":{…}}; the tool fills
                            signer, seq, prev, ts and sig
  --kind <kind>             authored, holds, describes or retracts (without
                            --draft)
  --content <file>          the bytes the statement is about; installed in the
                            store with the statements
  --media-type <type>       content.media_type
  --type <type>             authored type (default dataset)
  --provenance <json>       authored provenance object {workflow, inputs[,
                            environment]}
  --no-holds                authored or observed only: keep the bytes in the
                            store but write no holds (a withheld artifact)

Describes and retracts options:
  --about <digest|id>       describes: the blob or statement described
  --note <text>             describes: the note
  --of <id>                 retracts: the statement retracted
  --reason <text>           retracts: the reason

Acting-for-identity options:
  --as <identity>           act for a fixed-root identity (its inception id):
                            the statement is written rc/2 with x_actor_v1
                            {identity, grant}; needs --grant
  --grant <id>              with --as: the grant (its statement id) your key
                            acts under
  --amends <id[,id…]>       with --as, authored or observed only: the identity's
                            earlier claims this one amends (identity profile §5)

Authored disclosure options:
  --attribution <text>      authored disclosure attribution
  --availability <json>     authored disclosure availability
  --comparator <digest>     authored disclosure comparator
  --description <text>      authored disclosure description
  --extends <digest>        authored disclosure extends
  --fulfills <json>         authored disclosure fulfills
  --intended-for <resource/edition>
                            run the edition's rules on the bytes before they are
                            pinned (your own policy, chosen by naming the
                            edition); fail refuses, ok or unchecked is recorded
  --licence <text>          authored disclosure licence
  --licence-texts <json>    authored disclosure licence_texts
  --pre-registration <text>
                            authored disclosure pre_registration
  --replication <text>      authored disclosure replication
  --sampling <json>         authored disclosure sampling
  --shelf-life-days <integer>
                            authored disclosure shelf_life_days
  --supersedes <digest>     authored disclosure supersedes

External signing options:
  --prepare <file>          do not sign: write an external-signing package
                            (rc-signing-package/1) to this new file: the
                            unsigned statement, the exact bytes to sign (PAE,
                            base64), the signer, the proposed id, your log's
                            head and a summary; needs --signer, takes no --key,
                            appends nothing; an existing file is never
                            overwritten
  --signer <key>            with --prepare: the public form of the key that will
                            sign (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); the algorithm
                            follows from the form
  --complete <file>         commit a package written by --prepare, with
                            --signature: the bytes signed are recomputed from
                            the package, the signature verified, every check of
                            direct signing run again; refused, nothing appended,
                            when your log moved since the package was prepared
                            (stale-prepare)
  --signature <file|->      with --complete: the signature over the package's
                            PAE bytes in the statement encoding (ed25519: 128
                            lowercase hex; eip191-secp256k1: 0x + 130 lowercase
                            hex); - reads standard input

Examples:
  commons publish --store store --key me.key --kind authored --content data.csv
      --media-type text/csv
